/**
 * @file server.js
 * @brief The Node.js back-end of the CloudAvid File Portal: a zero-framework HTTP server that serves the SPA shell, static assets, a filesystem-driven JSON API, and streamed downloads.
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_overview 📖 Overview
 *
 * @subsection server_eli5 🧒 ELI5 Summary
 *
 * Think of this file as the **reception desk** of a building.
 *
 * You (the browser) walk in the front door and say things like:
 * "I want the welcome brochure" (the `index.html` page), or "Can I see the
 * list of rooms?" (`/api/categories`), or "Please hand me the big box in room
 * 3, shelf 2" (`/api/download/...`). The receptionist — this file — never
 * builds the rooms itself and never touches the boxes directly. It just
 * listens to what you ask for, checks that you are allowed to have it, and
 * then calls the right **specialist** (the modules in `lib/`) who does the
 * actual walking-and-fetching. If you ask for something dangerous — like
 * "give me the staff-only key cabinet" (`/lib/`, `/.env`) — the receptionist
 * politely but firmly says "403 Forbidden" and does not even look inside.
 *
 * @subsection server_responsibilities Core Responsibilities
 *
 * - **HTTP Endpoint:** Creates the single Node.js `http.Server` for the whole
 *   project and binds it to `0.0.0.0` on the configured port. It is the only
 *   network listener in the repository.
 * - **Environment Bootstrap:** Loads `process.env` from the project-root
 *   `.env` file (via `lib/load-env.js`) *before* anything else reads it.
 * - **JSON API Router:** Implements six API endpoints (`/api/health`,
 *   `/api/categories`, `/api/releases`, `/api/search`, `/api/files`,
 *   `/api/download`) by delegating all filesystem work to `lib/files-service.js`.
 * - **SPA Fallback Server:** Returns `index.html` for `/`, `/browse`, and any
 *   unknown HTML-ish route, so client-side routing (`assets/js/router.js`)
 *   works on refresh and deep links.
 * - **Static Asset Server:** Serves `assets/`, fonts, icons and other files
 *   from the project root with a curated MIME table, a one-hour cache policy,
 *   and an inline SVG placeholder for missing images.
 * - **Security Gatekeeper:** Blocks path traversal, blocks access to
 *   `/.env`, `/lib/`, `/node_modules/`, sets `nosniff` / `SAMEORIGIN` /
 *   `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` headers on every served response, and
 *   sanitises the `Content-Disposition` filename of downloads.
 * - **Cross-Origin Enabler:** Answers CORS preflights (`OPTIONS`) and stamps
 *   `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` on every JSON and download response,
 *   because `nginx.conf` proxies only the `/api/` prefix and other origins
 *   may call the API directly.
 * - **Ops Surface:** Colour-coded request logging, a startup banner that
 *   reports the health of `FILES_ROOT`, a health endpoint for uptime
 *   monitors, and a graceful `SIGINT` shutdown handler.
 *
 * @subsection server_arch_role 🧭 Role in the Overall Architecture
 *
 * `server.js` is the **front door of the back-end**. It contains *zero*
 * filesystem logic of its own; every read, stat, walk and safety check on
 * the downloads tree is delegated to `lib/files-service.js`. This is a
 * deliberate two-layer split:
 *
 * - **server.js** = transport layer (HTTP, headers, streaming, CORS, routing).
 * - **lib/files-service.js** = domain layer (FILES_ROOT resolution, path
 *   sanitisation, directory walking, search, release ranking).
 *
 * The frontend mirror of this split is `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`,
 * which is the *only* frontend file allowed to call this server's API.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_architecture 🏗️ Architecture: How A Request Flows Through This File
 *
 * The diagram below shows every decision point of the main request handler.
 * Note the deliberate ordering: URL parse → CORS preflight → method filter →
 * security blocklist → API routes → SPA routes → static fallback → error net.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph request_flow {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   request  [label="http.createServer\\nasync (req, res)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   parse    [label="new URL(req.url) +\\ndecodeURIComponent(pathname)\\n400 on failure", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   options  [label="OPTIONS?\\n→ 204 + CORS preflight headers", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   method   [label="Method not GET/HEAD?\\n→ 405 Method Not Allowed", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   blocked  [label="/.env · /lib/ · /node_modules/?\\n→ 403 Forbidden", fillcolor="#FEE2E2"];
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_api {
 *     label="JSON API (delegates to lib/files-service.js)";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;
 *
 *     health     [label="/api/health · /__health\\npeekRootSync + JSON status", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     categories [label="/api/categories\\nlistCategories()", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     releases   [label="/api/releases?limit=\\nlistLatestReleases()", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     search     [label="/api/search?q=&limit=\\nsearchEntries()", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     files      [label="/api/files[/rel] ?path=\\nlistPath()", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *     download   [label="/api/download[/rel] ?path=\\ngetDownloadTarget() → streamDownload()", fillcolor="#FCE7F3"];
 *   }
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_pages {
 *     label="Pages & Static Assets";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF"; fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=11;
 *
 *     spa_route  [label="/ · /index.html ·\\n/browse · /browse/*\\n(assettish ext → static, else SPA)", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *     serveSPA   [label="serveSPA()\\nreads index.html\\n+ security headers", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *     serveStatic[label="serveStatic(pathname)\\nMIME + cache + traversal guard\\nmissing image → SVG placeholder\\nmissing html → SPA fallback", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   }
 *
 *   error  [label="catch (err)\\nsendServiceError()\\nerr.httpStatus or 500", fillcolor="#FEE2E2"];
 *   client [label="Browser / curl / uptime monitor", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *
 *   request -> parse;
 *   parse -> options;
 *   options -> method;
 *   method -> blocked;
 *   blocked -> health;
 *   blocked -> categories;
 *   blocked -> releases;
 *   blocked -> search;
 *   blocked -> files;
 *   blocked -> download;
 *   blocked -> spa_route;
 *
 *   health -> client [label="JSON"];
 *   categories -> client [label="JSON"];
 *   releases -> client [label="JSON"];
 *   search -> client [label="JSON"];
 *   files -> client [label="JSON"];
 *   download -> client [label="binary stream"];
 *   spa_route -> serveSPA [label="no file extension"];
 *   spa_route -> serveStatic [label="has MIMES extension"];
 *   serveSPA -> client [label="HTML"];
 *   serveStatic -> client [label="asset / 404 / SVG / SPA"];
 *   serveStatic -> serveSPA [label=".html or extensionless\\nfile missing", style=dashed];
 *   spa_route -> error [label="service error", style=dashed];
 *   download -> error [label="e.g. PATH_TRAVERSAL", style=dashed];
 *   parse -> error [style=dashed];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * The dependency graph below shows how `server.js` connects the whole project:
 * it consumes the environment layer, delegates the domain work, and is
 * consumed by nginx (in production), npm start (in development), and the
 * frontend's single API client.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph dependencies {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_env {
 *     label="Environment";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF";
 *     dotenv_dep [label="dotenv npm package\\n(preferred .env loader)", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *     load_env   [label="lib/load-env.js\\nloadEnv() + fallback parser", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *     env_file   [label=".env · .env.example\\nPORT, FILES_ROOT", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   }
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_server {
 *     label="This file";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF";
 *     server [label="server.js\\nHTTP transport + routing", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   }
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_domain {
 *     label="Domain layer";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF";
 *     files_service [label="lib/files-service.js\\nlistCategories · listPath\\ngetDownloadTarget · searchEntries\\nlistLatestReleases · peekRootSync", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *   }
 *
 *   subgraph cluster_consumers {
 *     label="Consumers";
 *     style="dashed"; color="#9CA3AF";
 *     nginx  [label="nginx.conf\\nlocation /api/ → 127.0.0.1:3000", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *     npm    [label="package.json\\n\"start\": \"node server.js\"", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *     ressvc [label="assets/js/services/resource-service.js\\nfetch() /api/*", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *     auto   [label="assets/js/autocomplete.js\\nsuggestions via ResourceService.search", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *     pages  [label="assets/js/home.js · browse.js\\nlists + download links", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *   }
 *
 *   env_file -> load_env;
 *   dotenv_dep -> load_env [label="preferred, optional"];
 *   load_env -> server [label="loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)"];
 *   server -> files_service [label="6 API handlers delegate"];
 *   nginx -> server [label="reverse proxy"];
 *   npm -> server [label="npm start"];
 *   ressvc -> server [label="GET /api/categories · files · search · releases · download"];
 *   ressvc -> auto;
 *   ressvc -> pages;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_header 🧩 File Header Comment
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * The very first lines of `server.js`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * This is the name-tag on the reception desk. It tells a human skimming the
 * repository what this file is and on which operating systems it runs. It
 * carries no executable meaning — it is purely documentation inside code.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Pure comment; no runtime effect. The claims it makes ("filesystem-driven",
 * "Windows + Linux") are backed by `lib/files-service.js` (which normalises
 * separators with `path.*` calls) and by this file's `path.join` /
 * `path.normalize` usage throughout.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The `═══` banner style is the project's house convention for module
 * headers; the same style opens `lib/files-service.js`... and, on the
 * frontend, `assets/js/resource-service.js`, `assets/js/router.js` and
 * `assets/js/autocomplete.js`.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * /* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *    server.js — CloudAvid File Portal
 *    Filesystem-driven HTTP server (Windows + Linux)
 *    ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
 * ```
 *
 * @warning When editing, keep this banner comment syntactically closed. If
 * the closing delimiter (`*` immediately followed by `/`) is lost, the
 * `require("http")` below it becomes part of
 * the comment and the server crashes on startup with `http is not defined`.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_requires 🧩 Block: Core Module Requires (http, fs, path, URL)
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * Immediately after the header banner.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * Before the receptionist can work, they need their tools: a telephone
 * (`http`) to talk to visitors, a ladder into the file archive (`fs`), a map
 * of the building (`path`), and a magnifying glass to read the name tag on
 * every request (`URL`).
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * All four are **Node.js built-ins** — this is the reason the project's only
 * runtime dependency (`dotenv`, see `package.json`) can be treated as
 * optional: even if `node_modules` were wiped, the server would still start,
 * merely losing the polished `.env` parsing. `URL` is destructured from
 * `require("url")` instead of using the global `URL`, which keeps the file
 * explicit and works identically on old Node versions where the global was
 * not yet exposed.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * - `http` powers `http.createServer` and `server.listen` at the bottom.
 * - `fs` powers `fs.readFile` in `serveSPA`/`serveStatic` and
 *   `fs.createReadStream` in `streamDownload`.
 * - `path` powers every safe-path helper (`isInsideRoot`, `serveStatic`,
 *   `serveSPA`, `streamDownload`'s extension lookup).
 * - `URL` powers the request parsing inside the main handler.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The frontend equivalent of "requires" are the ordered `<script defer>`
 * tags in `index.html`; the same "standard library first" spirit appears in
 * `lib/load-env.js` and `lib/files-service.js`, which also open with plain
 * built-in requires and no third-party imports.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const http = require("http");
 * const fs = require("fs");
 * const path = require("path");
 * const { URL } = require("url");
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * If you swap `require("fs")` for `fs/promises` here, the four callback-style
 * `fs.readFile(..., (err, data) => ...)` call sites stop working silently at
 * runtime (they would receive a Promise, never the buffer). Both `lib/`
 * modules already use promises, but this file intentionally does not.
 *
 * @see lib/load-env.js
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see package.json
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_paths 🧩 Block: PROJECT_ROOT and Environment Bootstrap
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * Right after the requires.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * This is the receptionist pinning the building map on the wall. `PROJECT_ROOT`
 * says "everything I serve lives here", and `loadEnv` pastes the office
 * rulebook (`.env`) onto the wall so every later decision — which port, where
 * the downloads live — can read it.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * `__dirname` is the directory containing `server.js`, i.e. the repository
 * root in this project's layout. `loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)` therefore resolves
 * `<project root>/.env`. The call happens **synchronously at module top
 * level, before `files-service` is required** — this ordering is load-bearing:
 * `PORT` below and `FILES_ROOT` (read lazily by `lib/files-service.js`) both
 * assume `process.env` is already populated. `loadEnv` prefers the `dotenv`
 * package and silently falls back to a hand-rolled parser if it is not
 * installed, and it never overwrites an existing environment variable.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - File on disk: `.env` (template: `.env.example` — keys `PORT`, `FILES_ROOT`).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - Populated `process.env.PORT` and `process.env.FILES_ROOT` for the whole
 *   Node process.
 * - `PROJECT_ROOT` constant used by every API handler and passed as the first
 *   argument to every `lib/files-service.js` call.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * - Depends on `lib/load-env.js` → `loadEnv()`, which depends on the `dotenv`
 *   package (optional) and `fs`/`path`.
 * - Depends on `node_modules/dotenv` being *installed* (it is declared in
 *   `package.json`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * - `.env.example` documents the two keys this bootstrap makes real; DevOps
 *   copy it to `.env` on deploy.
 * - `nginx.conf` deliberately does **not** configure `FILES_ROOT` — its
 *   comment says "FILES_ROOT is configured in the Node process .env — not in
 *   this file", i.e. this bootstrap is the single source of truth.
 * - `package.json`'s `"start": "node server.js"` is the command that triggers
 *   this whole sequence.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The "pass `PROJECT_ROOT` into the service" convention is the reason
 * `lib/files-service.js` can stay testable and location-agnostic: every
 * exported function there (`listCategories`, `listPath`, `getDownloadTarget`,
 * `searchEntries`, `listLatestReleases`, `peekRootSync`) takes
 * `projectRoot` as its first parameter instead of importing a global.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const PROJECT_ROOT = __dirname;
 * const { loadEnv } = require("./lib/load-env");
 * loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT);
 *
 * const filesService = require("./lib/files-service");
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Reordering these lines — e.g. requiring `lib/files-service.js` before
 * calling `loadEnv` — still works today only because `files-service` reads
 * `FILES_ROOT` lazily inside each request. But if `files-service` ever gains
 * top-level `process.env.FILES_ROOT` capture (a natural "optimisation"),
 * that reordering would make every request 503 with `FILES_ROOT_MISSING`.
 * Treat the call order here as a contract, not a style choice. Renaming
 * `PROJECT_ROOT` also breaks nothing internally, but any new file that
 * mirrors this pattern should keep the same name for greppability.
 *
 * @see lib/load-env.js
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see .env.example
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_hostport 🧩 Block: HOST, PORT and ROOT Constants
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * Directly after the files-service require.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The desk's address: which door to open (`HOST`), which number plate to hang
 * on it (`PORT`), and a nickname for the building (`ROOT`).
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * - `HOST = "0.0.0.0"` binds to **all** interfaces. That is what allows
 *   `nginx.conf` to reach this process over the loopback interface
 *   (`proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;`) without any socket tricks. It also
 *   means the port is reachable from the network, so the security blocklist
 *   later in the request handler is not merely theoretical.
 * - `PORT` reads `process.env.PORT` with a `3000` fallback. Note the truthiness
 *   form: `PORT=0` in `.env` would fall through to 3000 rather than picking a
 *   random OS port — an edge case worth knowing before "fixing" it.
 * - `ROOT = PROJECT_ROOT` is an alias kept purely for readability at the call
 *   sites (`isInsideRoot(ROOT, fp)`, `path.join(ROOT, ...)`, `path.join(ROOT,
 *   "index.html")`).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `process.env.PORT` (set by the `.env` bootstrap above; documented in
 *   `.env.example` as "HTTP port for the Node.js server").
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * - The listen target used by `server.listen(PORT, HOST, ...)`.
 * - The `http://localhost:${PORT}` base used by `decodePathname` and by the
 *   `new URL(req.url, ...)` parsing in the main handler.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * `nginx.conf` hardcodes the expectation of this port in `proxy_pass
 * http://127.0.0.1:3000`. Changing the default `3000` here without editing
 * `nginx.conf` (or setting `PORT` in `.env` to match) silently 502s the whole
 * production site.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * `PORT` also appears in `lib/load-env.js`'s domain (via `.env`) and in the
 * printed URLs of the startup banner. The frontend never needs to know it:
 * every fetch in `assets/js/services/resource-service.js` uses *relative*
 * URLs (`"/api/categories"`), which is exactly what makes the nginx proxy
 * transparent.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const HOST = "0.0.0.0";
 * const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
 * const ROOT = PROJECT_ROOT;
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Changing `HOST` to `"127.0.0.1"` for "safety" is a behaviour change: in
 * container deployments that port-map the service, health checks and the
 * nginx upstream must both agree with the new bind address. Changing the
 * default port breaks `nginx.conf` (see above) and the instructions printed
 * in the startup banner.
 *
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see .env.example
 * @see index.html
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_colors 🧩 Block: The ANSI Colour Palette `c`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After the host/port constants.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * A box of highlighter pens for the terminal. Each pen is a tiny invisible
 * code that makes the next text a colour, and `reset` is the cap that stops
 * the colour bleeding onto everything after it.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Plain string map of ANSI escape sequences — no `chalk`, no dependency. The
 * keys are used in exactly three places: `log()` (status colouring), and the
 * startup banner + `SIGINT` handler (cyan/yellow/green emphasis). Because
 * these are raw escapes, they are emitted even when stdout is a redirected
 * file or pipe; ANSI-aware log shippers ignore them, and non-ANSI consumers
 * see a few harmless control characters.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * None — a constant.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Colour-wrapped fragments interpolated into template literals in
 * `log()`, `server.listen`'s banner, and the shutdown message.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * Dependency-free "small utility" is a house pattern: `lib/load-env.js`
 * hand-rolls `.env` parsing for the same reason. The palette has no
 * counterpart on the frontend — browser logs there are uncoloured.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const c = {
 *     reset: "\x1b[0m",
 *     dim: "\x1b[2m",
 *     green: "\x1b[32m",
 *     yellow: "\x1b[33m",
 *     cyan: "\x1b[36m",
 *     red: "\x1b[31m",
 *     magenta: "\x1b[35m",
 *     bold: "\x1b[1m",
 * };
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Deleting an unused-looking key (`magenta` is currently referenced by no
 * call site, but `dim`, `bold`, `green`, `yellow`, `cyan` and `red` all are)
 * is safe *today* only if you re-check `log()` and the banner after any
 * refactor. If `c` itself is removed, `log()` and `server.listen` throw
 * `ReferenceError: c is not defined` on the first request — taking down the
 * server exactly when it starts receiving traffic.
 *
 * @see lib/load-env.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_mimes 🧩 Block: The MIME Table `MIMES`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After the colour palette.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * This is the sticker machine at the shipping counter. Every file that leaves
 * the building gets a sticker saying what it *is* — a picture, a web page, a
 * zip — so the receiver's browser knows whether to display it, play it, or
 * offer a download.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * An extension → `Content-Type` map consulted in three places:
 * 1. `serveStatic` — picks the header for static assets (`MIMES[ext] ||
 *    "application/octet-stream"`).
 * 2. `streamDownload` — same fallback for downloads, so a `.iso` arrives as
 *    `application/octet-stream` and a `.pdf` as `application/pdf` even though
 *    `Content-Disposition: attachment` forces the save dialog anyway.
 * 3. The SPA route — the odd one out: `if (ext && MIMES[ext] && ext !==
 *    ".html")` uses the table as an *extension whitelist* to decide whether a
 *    `/browse/...`-style path is a real asset (serve statically) or a client
 *    route (serve the SPA shell).
 * Text entries carry `charset=utf-8`; binary entries deliberately do not.
 * Note the table doubles as the project's "supported download catalogue":
 * `.iso`, `.qcow2`, `.vmdk`, `.docx`, `.vsdx` are here because this portal
 * distributes VM images and Office/Visio documents.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * None — a constant.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * `Content-Type` header values for every static and download response; an
 * extension-membership test for the SPA-vs-static routing decision.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * The extensions listed here are the ones the frontend will happily link to:
 * `assets/js/router.js` keeps its own `fileExtensions` regex (html, zip, tar,
 * gz, iso, qcow2, vmdk, pdf, txt, log, sql, docx, xlsx, pptx, png, jpg, exec,
 * sh, yml, yaml, md, json, csv) to decide which links must *not* be
 * SPA-navigated but downloaded/navigated natively. The two lists overlap on
 * purpose but are not identical — adding a new extension here (e.g. `.deb`)
 * makes the server serve it correctly, while adding it to `router.js`'s regex
 * is what stops the SPA from hijacking clicks on such links.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * `serveStatic` also uses `ext` to pick the cache policy
 * (`no-cache` for `.html`, `public, max-age=3600` for everything else) and to
 * decide whether a missing file should become the inline SVG placeholder
 * (images list) or the SPA fallback (`.html` / extensionless).
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The same "map instead of library" minimalism as the `c` palette. The
 * frontend mirrors the pattern conceptually with `inferIcon()` in
 * `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`, which maps *filename patterns* to
 * icon categories.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const MIMES = {
 *     ".html": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".css": "text/css; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".js": "text/javascript; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".mjs": "text/javascript; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".json": "application/json; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".png": "image/png",
 *     ".jpg": "image/jpeg",
 *     ".jpeg": "image/jpeg",
 *     ".webp": "image/webp",
 *     ".svg": "image/svg+xml",
 *     ".ico": "image/x-icon",
 *     ".woff": "font/woff",
 *     ".woff2": "font/woff2",
 *     ".ttf": "font/ttf",
 *     ".map": "application/json",
 *     ".txt": "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".pdf": "application/pdf",
 *     ".iso": "application/octet-stream",
 *     ".zip": "application/zip",
 *     ".gz": "application/gzip",
 *     ".qcow2": "application/octet-stream",
 *     ".vmdk": "application/octet-stream",
 *     ".sql": "application/sql",
 *     ".log": "text/plain; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".yml": "text/yaml; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".yaml": "text/yaml; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".sh": "text/x-shellscript",
 *     ".md": "text/markdown; charset=utf-8",
 *     ".docx": "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document",
 *     ".vsdx": "application/vnd.ms-visio.drawing",
 * };
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This table is consumed by `serveStatic`, `streamDownload` **and** the SPA
 * route guard. Adding an entry is additive and safe. Removing or renaming an
 * entry silently changes behaviour in three places at once: e.g. deleting
 * `".svg"` makes SVG assets download as octet-streams *and* makes
 * `/browse/logo.svg` fall through to the SPA shell instead of the static
 * handler. Keep it in sync with the `fileExtensions` regex in
 * `assets/js/router.js` and the image-extension list inside `serveStatic`.
 *
 * @see assets/js/router.js
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see nginx.conf
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_log 🧩 Function: `log(method, status, url, extra)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * Between the MIME table and `sendJSON`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * After every visitor interaction, the receptionist writes one line in the
 * day-book: *time, what they asked, and how it went* — written in green when
 * everything went fine, yellow when something was their mistake, red when
 * the building itself is on fire.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Picks a colour from the `c` palette by status band (`≥500` red, `≥400`
 * yellow, `≥300` cyan, else green), timestamps with a `HH:MM:SS` slice of the
 * ISO date, and pads the method to 6 characters with `padEnd(6)` so columns
 * line up. Called from every terminal branch of the request handler and from
 * the blocklist guard, always *after* the response has been produced, so a
 * logging bug can never corrupt a response.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `method` — e.g. `"GET"`, `"HEAD"`.
 * - `status` — numeric HTTP status.
 * - `url` — the decoded pathname.
 * - `extra` — optional suffix: latency (`${Date.now() - start}ms`), item
 *   counts (`${categories.length} categories · …`), service error codes.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * One `console.log` line per handled request. No file logging — `nginx.conf`
 * disables `access_log` for static assets and expects the Node process's
 * stdout to be captured by the platform service manager.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Consumed exclusively inside this file by the main request handler, the
 * blocklist branch, and every API/SPA/static branch (always with the
 * `Date.now() - start` latency suffix).
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The frontend has a different observability culture: `assets/js/services/
 * performance-monitor.js` measures client-side performance instead. Server
 * latency strings here ("… · 3ms") are the human-readable counterpart of the
 * `Cache-Control: no-store` headers the API sends — together they say "the
 * data is fresh and fast".
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function log(method, status, url, extra = "") {
 *     const color =
 *         status >= 500 ? c.red :
 *             status >= 400 ? c.yellow :
 *                 status >= 300 ? c.cyan :
 *                     c.green;
 *     const time = new Date().toISOString().slice(11, 19);
 *     console.log(
 *         `${c.dim}${time}${c.reset} ${c.bold}${method.padEnd(6)}${c.reset} ${color}${status}${c.reset} ${url} ${c.dim}${extra}${c.reset}`
 *     );
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Change the *signature* and every call site in this file must change too.
 * Change the *format* and anything tailing stdout (log shippers, `journalctl`
 * filters, alerting regexes on status codes) loses its anchors. Do not route
 * `log()` through anything that can throw synchronously — it runs inside the
 * request handler's `try`, so a throwing logger would convert successful
 * responses into the 500 path of `sendServiceError`.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see assets/js/services/performance-monitor.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_sendjson 🧩 Function: `sendJSON(res, status, data, headers)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `log`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The standard envelope the receptionist puts answers in: a clean white
 * envelope (JSON), the right stamp (`Content-Type`), the exact weight
 * (`Content-Length`), and a sticker saying "anyone may open this"
 * (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`).
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Serialises with `JSON.stringify(data, null, 0)` (compact), writes a header
 * set with sensible API defaults, then spreads the caller's `headers` last so
 * a call site can override any default. Every API handler in the main router
 * funnels through this function; `sendServiceError` uses it too, which is how
 * error payloads inherit the same CORS/caching policy as successes.
 * `Content-Length` is computed with `Buffer.byteLength(body)` — correct for
 * multi-byte UTF-8 payloads (e.g. non-ASCII filenames in search results),
 * which `body.length` would get wrong.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `res` — the ServerResponse.
 * - `status` — HTTP status code.
 * - `data` — any JSON-serialisable value (arrays for listings, objects for
 *   health/errors).
 * - `headers` — optional per-call overrides (currently unused by call sites,
 *   kept for future needs).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Default headers written:
 * `"Content-Type": "application/json; charset=utf-8"`, `"Content-Length"`,
 * `"Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*"`,
 * `"Access-Control-Allow-Methods": "GET, HEAD, OPTIONS"`,
 * `"Access-Control-Allow-Headers": "Content-Type"`,
 * `"Cache-Control": "no-store"` — then the body.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * The most important consumer is `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`:
 * its `getCachedData`/`setCachedData` implement their *own* 5-minute
 * `sessionStorage` cache precisely because this endpoint sends
 * `Cache-Control: no-store` (the HTTP cache is told never to help). Its
 * `parseError()` also depends on this function's shape: `res.json()` is
 * expected to yield `{ error: "..." }` for non-OK statuses.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on nothing outside Node core; it *is* a dependency of every API
 * branch below and of `sendServiceError`.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * `sendText` is its text-bodied sibling with a reduced header set; both
 * exist so `HEAD`/static/error paths never accidentally inherit the JSON
 * content type.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function sendJSON(res, status, data, headers = {}) {
 *     const body = JSON.stringify(data, null, 0);
 *     res.writeHead(status, {
 *         "Content-Type": "application/json; charset=utf-8",
 *         "Content-Length": Buffer.byteLength(body),
 *         "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*",
 *         "Access-Control-Allow-Methods": "GET, HEAD, OPTIONS",
 *         "Access-Control-Allow-Headers": "Content-Type",
 *         "Cache-Control": "no-store",
 *         ...headers,
 *     });
 *     res.end(body);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is the **API contract writer**. Narrowing `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`
 * from `*` to a specific origin breaks any direct (non-nginx-proxied) API
 * consumer. Removing `Cache-Control: no-store` turns the frontend's
 * `sessionStorage` + browser-cache combination into a double cache whose
 * invalidation you do not control. Changing the error payload shape
 * (`{ error, code }`, see `sendServiceError`) breaks `parseError()` in
 * `resource-service.js` and the user-visible error messages of the entire
 * portal.
 *
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/home.js
 * @see assets/js/browse.js
 * @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_sendtext 🧩 Function: `sendText(res, status, text, contentType)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `sendJSON`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The sticky-note version of an answer: short, plain text, no envelope. Used
 * when there is nothing structured to say — "Forbidden", "404 — whatever you
 * typed", "Bad Request".
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Writes only `Content-Type` (defaulting to `text/plain; charset=utf-8`,
 * overridable) and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, then the body. Note the
 * deliberate differences from `sendJSON`: **no `Content-Length`** (Node uses
 * chunked encoding), and **no `Cache-Control`** (errors are cacheable per
 * HTTP defaults, which is acceptable for these never-changing messages).
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `res`, `status`, `text`, optional `contentType`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * A plain-text response body with CORS enabled.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Called from: URL-parse failure (400), method guard (405), the security
 * blocklist (403), `serveStatic` (403 traversal, 404 missing asset),
 * `serveSPA` (500 when `index.html` is missing), `streamDownload` (500 when
 * the read stream errors before headers are sent). It is this file's
 * universal "say something honest, quickly" path.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * Frontend error handling rarely surfaces these raw strings: for API calls,
 * `resource-service.js`'s `parseError()` prefers the JSON `{error}` body from
 * `sendServiceError`; for page navigation, `router.js` simply lets the
 * browser display the text.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function sendText(res, status, text, contentType = "text/plain; charset=utf-8") {
 *     res.writeHead(status, {
 *         "Content-Type": contentType,
 *         "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*",
 *     });
 *     res.end(text);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Adding `Cache-Control` headers here would change how browsers and upstream
 * proxies (nginx in production) cache *error* responses — with a long
 * max-age, a single transient 500 for `index.html` could stick at the proxy.
 * Removing the CORS header breaks browser-visible error text for cross-origin
 * API consumers. Keep this function boring; it is the last line of defence
 * when everything else has already gone wrong.
 *
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_senderror 🧩 Function: `sendServiceError(res, err)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `sendText`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The translator between the specialists in the back office and the visitor.
 * When a specialist hands the receptionist a slip saying "this shelf does not
 * exist" or "you may not go in there", this turns the slip into a proper,
 * polite JSON answer with the right number on it.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Reads two *duck-typed* properties that `lib/files-service.js`'s
 * `serviceError()` attaches to plain `Error` objects: `err.httpStatus`
 * (default 500) and `err.code` (default `"INTERNAL"`). Responds with
 * `{ error: err.message || "Unexpected error", code: err.code || "INTERNAL" }`.
 * The `||` defaults make the function safe for *unexpected* errors (a real
 * `TypeError`, an fs error without annotations) as well as expected service
 * errors — this is the single convergence point of the main handler's
 * `catch (err)`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `err` — any thrown value; service errors from `lib/files-service.js`
 *   carry `{ httpStatus, code, message }`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Via `sendJSON`: e.g. `503 { "error": "FILES_ROOT does not exist…",
 * "code": "FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND" }` or `403 { "error": "Requested path is
 * outside FILES_ROOT.", "code": "PATH_TRAVERSAL" }`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * - `assets/js/services/resource-service.js` → `parseError(res)` reads
 *   `body.error` and throws it as a JS `Error` message; that message is
 *   ultimately displayed to users by `home.js` and `browse.js` in their
 *   error cards.
 * - The `code` values (`FILES_ROOT_MISSING`, `FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND`,
 *   `FILES_ROOT_NOT_DIRECTORY`, `FILES_ROOT_EMPTY`, `FILES_ROOT_NO_CATEGORIES`,
 *   `INVALID_PATH`, `PATH_TRAVERSAL`, `NOT_FOUND`, `NOT_A_DIRECTORY`,
 *   `NOT_A_FILE`) are defined in `lib/files-service.js` and are also echoed
 *   into the request log line via `err.code || err.message`.
 * - The health endpoint complements this: instead of raising these errors it
 *   *reports* the FILES_ROOT state as `"degraded"` via `peekRootSync`, so
 *   uptime monitors can alert before users see the errors.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * This is the same "transport maps domain errors to HTTP" pattern used by
 * every serious Node codebase; here it is 6 lines because the domain layer
 * already annotates its errors. Nothing in the frontend distinguishes
 * `error` from `code` today except display — but `code` is the stable
 * machine-readable field for future logic.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function sendServiceError(res, err) {
 *     const status = err.httpStatus || 500;
 *     sendJSON(res, status, {
 *         error: err.message || "Unexpected error",
 *         code: err.code || "INTERNAL",
 *     });
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The property names `httpStatus` and `code` are a **cross-file contract**
 * with `lib/files-service.js`'s `serviceError()`. Renaming either side breaks
 * error handling invisibly — every domain error would become a generic 500
 * `INTERNAL` while tests using only success paths stay green. Changing the
 * JSON keys (`error`, `code`) breaks `parseError()` in
 * `resource-service.js` and therefore every error message the UI shows.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/home.js
 * @see assets/js/browse.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_sechdrs 🧩 Function: `securityHeaders(res)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `sendServiceError`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * Three safety stickers glued onto every page and asset leaving the building:
 * "don't guess what I am" (nosniff), "you may frame me only from my own
 * house" (SAMEORIGIN), and "when people mention where they came from, be
 * discreet" (referrer policy).
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Uses `res.setHeader` (merge-patch semantics, applied before
 * `res.writeHead`) rather than passing headers to `writeHead`, so it can be
 * composed with any later `writeHead` without collision. Called by
 * `serveSPA` (both success and via `serveStatic`'s fallback) and by the
 * success path of `serveStatic`. It is intentionally *not* called by
 * `streamDownload`, which sets its own strict subset (`X-Content-Type-Options`
 * inline) — a download is never framed or navigated, only saved.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `res` — the ServerResponse.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Response headers: `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`,
 * `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`, `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * `nginx.conf` adds the *same* three headers (plus HSTS and
 * `X-XSS-Protection`) with `add_header ... always` at the server level.
 * Caution is warranted: nginx's `add_header` inheritance rules mean the
 * `location /api/` and static-asset blocks (which declare their own
 * `add_header`) do **not** inherit the server-level set — so this function is
 * what guarantees the headers on responses served directly by Node during
 * development, and duplicates them harmlessly for proxied API responses.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The security posture matches the frontend's: `index.html` pins theme/perf
 * state before paint (see `index.html`'s inline boot script) — the pair
 * "strict server headers + strict pre-paint boot" is the project's
 * defence-in-depth habit.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function securityHeaders(res) {
 *     res.setHeader("X-Content-Type-Options", "nosniff");
 *     res.setHeader("X-Frame-Options", "SAMEORIGIN");
 *     res.setHeader("Referrer-Policy", "strict-origin-when-cross-origin");
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Removing `nosniff` re-enables MIME-sniffing attacks on user-controlled
 * content served from `FILES_ROOT` (the downloads tree is user-curated!).
 * Changing `SAMEORIGIN` to `DENY` breaks any future legitimate embedding of
 * the portal. These headers are asserted twice in the deployment (here and in
 * `nginx.conf`); keep the two lists consistent or document the divergence.
 *
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see index.html
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_isinsideroot 🧩 Function: `isInsideRoot(rootDir, filePath)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `securityHeaders`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The "are you still inside the building?" checker. A visitor may wander to
 * room 3, shelf 2 — fine. But a request for "the basement of the building
 * across the street" (`../../etc/passwd`-style) must be answered with a
 * simple no.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * The canonical path-traversal test: `path.relative(rootDir, filePath)` yields
 * `""` when the paths are equal, a `../`-prefixed string when the target
 * escapes, or an absolute path on a different drive (Windows). The expression
 * `rel && !rel.startsWith("..") && !path.isAbsolute(rel)` therefore accepts
 * strictly-*inside* targets (rejecting the root itself via `rel &&`) — and
 * `serveStatic` re-admits the one exception (`index.html`) explicitly with
 * `path.resolve(fp) !== path.resolve(ROOT, "index.html")`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `rootDir` — always `ROOT` (the project root) in this file.
 * - `filePath` — a joined candidate path under test.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Boolean. Consumed once, in `serveStatic`'s guard, which answers 403
 * `"Forbidden"` when it fails.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends only on Node's `path` module and its platform-correct separators.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * It protects only the *static* handler. The API's downloads/listings are
 * guarded by a **second, independent** checker: `resolveInsideRoot()` in
 * `lib/files-service.js`, which additionally rejects `.`/`..`/NUL segments
 * *before* resolving and throws a typed `PATH_TRAVERSAL` service error.
 * Two checkers exist because the two layers protect different roots: this
 * one protects the *source tree* (project root), that one protects the
 * *content tree* (`FILES_ROOT`).
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * Defense-in-depth pairs: `serveStatic` also strips leading `../` sequences
 * with `path.normalize(...).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/, "")` before joining,
 * and nginx adds a third layer (`location ~ /\.` deny all) in production.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function isInsideRoot(rootDir, filePath) {
 *     const rel = path.relative(rootDir, filePath);
 *     return rel && !rel.startsWith("..") && !path.isAbsolute(rel);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * "Simplifying" this to `!rel.startsWith("..")` (dropping `rel &&`) would
 * make the *root itself* pass the check, which combined with the
 * `index.html` exception in `serveStatic` changes which paths are servable.
 * Loosening it at all means any static-asset request can read `server.js`,
 * `lib/*.js` and `.env`-adjacent files if the blocklist is ever bypassed —
 * it is the last pure-Node barrier in front of the source tree. Keep the
 * double-guard (`this` + `resolveInsideRoot`) intact.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see nginx.conf
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_decodepathname 🧩 Function: `decodePathname(rawUrl)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `isInsideRoot`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The name-tag reader. When a visitor's badge says `%D9%81%D8%A7...` (weird
 * symbols for a Persian folder name) or `my%20files`, this translates it into
 * what the folder is *actually* called on disk.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Builds a `URL` against a synthetic `http://localhost:${PORT}` base (so
 * request targets like `/api/files/x` parse even without an absolute URL),
 * takes `.pathname` (dropping the query string), and percent-decodes it once.
 * In the current code it is defined but the main handler performs the
 * equivalent parse inline — the function remains as the single, documented
 * recipe for "pathname decoding" and as a ready-made tool for refactors;
 * note it is referenced by neither call site today (verified by search), so
 * treat edits here as API hygiene, not hot-path changes.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `rawUrl` — `req.url`, e.g. `"/api/download/ISO%20Images/win11.iso"`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * A decoded pathname string, e.g. `/api/download/ISO Images/win11.iso`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Percent-encoded path segments come from the frontend's
 * `encodeRelPath()` in `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`, which maps
 * every path segment through `encodeURIComponent` — the two functions are
 * the encode/decode pair that make non-ASCII filenames work end-to-end
 * (including Persian category names, matching the portal's FA language
 * support in `assets/js/i18n.js`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on the global-ish `URL` constructor destructured in the requires
 * and on `PORT` for the base URL.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function decodePathname(rawUrl) {
 *     return decodeURIComponent(new URL(rawUrl, `http://localhost:${PORT}`).pathname);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Decoding is the vulnerable half of the encode/decode pair: applying it
 * twice (or using `decodeURIComponent` on the *whole* URL including the
 * query) would let `%2e%2e%2f` smuggle traversal sequences past naive
 * filters before the guards see them. Any caller must decode *before*
 * security checks and only once. If you wire this into the main handler,
 * wrap it in the existing `try` — malformed sequences throw `URIError` and
 * must surface as 400.
 *
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/i18n.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_relprefix 🧩 Function: `relFromPrefix(pathname, prefix)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `decodePathname`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The "cut here" scissors. The visitor says "/api/download/ISOs/win11.iso";
 * the receptionist cuts off the "/api/download" label and hands the rest —
 * "ISOs/win11.iso" — to the specialist.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * `pathname.slice(prefix.length)` removes the prefix verbatim, then
 * `replace(/^\/+/, "")` strips any leading slashes so the service receives a
 * clean relative path. Used by exactly two branches of the router:
 * `/api/files/...` and `/api/download/...`, in their *path-segment* form
 * (as opposed to their `?path=` query form, which skips this helper). Note
 * the prefix slicing relies on the decoded pathname matching the prefix
 * byte-for-byte — spaces and Unicode are fine, but a prefix that itself
 * contained percent-encodings would not match.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `pathname` — decoded pathname.
 * - `prefix` — `"/api/files"` or `"/api/download"`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Relative path string (possibly `""` for `/api/files/` with no tail), which
 * is passed as `relPath` to `filesService.listPath` / `getDownloadTarget`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * The counterpart encoder is `encodeRelPath()` in
 * `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`; links are produced there by
 * `browseHref()` (→ `/browse/...`, SPA routes) and `downloadHref()` (→
 * `/api/download/...`, these endpoints) — and `autocomplete.js` builds
 * `<li data-href>` items from the same helpers.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function relFromPrefix(pathname, prefix) {
 *     const rest = pathname.slice(prefix.length);
 *     return rest.replace(/^\/+/, "");
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This helper is what defines the *URL grammar* of the API. If the router's
 * `pathname.startsWith("/api/files/")` branches change (e.g. you add
 * `/api/v2/files`), every branch and this helper must be updated together,
 * otherwise `slice()` will cut at the wrong offset and the service will
 * receive a corrupted `relPath` (surfacing as 404/400 from
 * `lib/files-service.js`). Frontend producers of these URLs live in
 * `resource-service.js` (`downloadHref`, `listFiles`) and must be updated in
 * the same commit.
 *
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_contentdisposition 🧩 Function: `contentDisposition(fileName)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `relFromPrefix`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The shipping label writer. When a big box (file download) leaves the
 * building, this writes the recipient's name on it twice: once in simple
 * letters everyone can read, and once in "international handwriting" so
 * names in any language (yes, even Persian!) survive the trip.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Implements RFC 5987/6266 dual-encoding for `Content-Disposition`:
 * 1. `fallback` — strips every character outside printable ASCII
 *    (`[^\x20-\x7E]` → `_`) and removes double quotes, producing a safe
 *    legacy `filename="..."` for ancient clients.
 * 2. `encoded` — the full `encodeURIComponent` form for the
 *    `filename*=UTF-8''...` variant understood by all modern browsers,
 *    preserving Unicode (e.g. Persian) filenames exactly.
 * Consumed only by `streamDownload`, which stamps it on every download
 * response so the browser *saves* the file instead of trying to render it.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `fileName` — the basename from `filesService.getDownloadTarget` (the last
 *   segment of the validated relative path).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * A complete header value, e.g.
 * `attachment; filename="win11_iso"; filename*=UTF-8''win11.iso`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Filenames arrive from `FILES_ROOT` (arbitrary, user-curated names — that is
 * the whole point of a file portal) after validation by
 * `lib/files-service.js`. The `attachment` disposition is the *third* layer of
 * download safety, alongside `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` (same header
 * block in `streamDownload`) and the `NOT_A_FILE` type check in the service.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Pure string manipulation — no dependencies beyond the JS standard library.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * On the client, `resource-service.js`'s `downloadHref()` builds the URL that
 * triggers this path, and `router.js`'s `fileExtensions` regex ensures the
 * SPA's click-interceptor stands aside for file links, letting the browser's
 * native download (and thus this header) do its job.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function contentDisposition(fileName) {
 *     const fallback = String(fileName).replace(/[^\x20-\x7E]/g, "_").replace(/"/g, "");
 *     const encoded = encodeURIComponent(fileName);
 *     return `attachment; filename="${fallback}"; filename*=UTF-8''${encoded}`;
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is a header-injection boundary. Removing the `.replace(/"/g, "")`
 * would allow a maliciously-named file (`x".html"; filename*=...`) to inject
 * extra header parameters. Changing `attachment` to `inline` turns the
 * downloads tree into a drive-by content-rendering surface (combined with
 * MIME types from `MIMES`). The regex is byte-literal — keep it exactly as
 * is unless you have a fuzz-tested replacement.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see assets/js/router.js
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_servespa 🧩 Function: `serveSPA(res)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `contentDisposition`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * Whenever someone asks for "a page of the app" — the lobby, or some deep
 * hallway whose exact room we don't need to check — the receptionist hands
 * them the *same* magic brochure (`index.html`). Inside the brochure, a
 * little guide (the SPA router) looks at where they wanted to go and walks
 * them there without ever leaving the brochure.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Reads `index.html` from disk on **every request** (no read-through cache:
 * `serveStatic` gives `.html` `Cache-Control: no-cache`, and this handler
 * sends no cache headers at all, so the browser must revalidate — an
 * intentional trade of a little latency for instant deploy propagation).
 * Error path answers 500 with an operator-focused message ("place it next to
 * server.js"). Success path stamps `securityHeaders(res)` *then* writes
 * `200` with `text/html; charset=utf-8`.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - File: `<project root>/index.html` — the SPA shell documented in
 *   `dox/main/index.dox`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * The full HTML shell; the browser then loads, in order, the CSS chain
 * (`tokens.css` → `base.css` → `components.css` → `home.css` → `browse.css`
 * → `performance-modes.css`) and the deferred JS chain
 * (`resource-service.js` → `performance-monitor.js` → `theme.js` →
 * `i18n.js` → `autocomplete.js` → `home.js` → `browse.js` → `router.js`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Invoked from two places: the explicit SPA branch (`/`, `/index.html`,
 * `/browse`, `/browse/...` — after its extension whitelist check) and
 * `serveStatic`'s fallback for missing `.html`/extensionless files.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on `fs.readFile`, `path.join`, `securityHeaders`, `sendText`, and
 * the presence of `index.html` on disk.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * This is the Node twin of nginx's `@spa` location in `nginx.conf`
 * (`rewrite ^ /index.html last;`): both exist so that deep links like
 * `/browse/ISOs/` survive refresh. In production nginx usually answers
 * first; this handler covers `npm start` development and any Node-served
 * deployment. The consumer of the shell is `assets/js/router.js`, whose
 * `resolveRoute()` understands exactly the same route vocabulary
 * (`/`, `/index.html` → home; `/browse`, `/browse/...` → browse).
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function serveSPA(res) {
 *     const indexPath = path.join(ROOT, "index.html");
 *     fs.readFile(indexPath, (err, data) => {
 *         if (err) {
 *             sendText(res, 500, "index.html missing — place it next to server.js");
 *             return;
 *         }
 *         securityHeaders(res);
 *         res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "text/html; charset=utf-8" });
 *         res.end(data);
 *     });
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Renaming or moving `index.html` breaks every SPA deep link *and* the
 * error message becomes a lie. Serving a different shell (e.g. the legacy
 * `browse.html`) would strand the deferred script chain in `index.html`
 * (`theme.js`, `performance-monitor.js`, `autocomplete.js` are loaded only
 * there) and silently disable theming, perf modes and search suggestions.
 * Also note the response carries no explicit cache header here — if you add
 * caching, coordinate with `serveStatic`'s `no-cache` policy for `.html`.
 *
 * @see index.html
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see assets/js/router.js
 * @see dox/main/index.dox
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_servestatic 🧩 Function: `serveStatic(reqPath, res)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `serveSPA`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The supply closet attendant. Every picture, stylesheet, script and font the
 * brochure mentions lives in this closet. You ask by name, the attendant
 * checks you're not asking for something outside the closet (no peeking into
 * the office!), finds the box, sticks the right label on it, and hands it
 * over. If a picture is missing, they hand you a friendly little "picture
 * not found" doodle instead of an error page. And if you ask for something
 * that isn't a closet item at all — you get the magic brochure again.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Four phases, in order:
 * 1. **Path sanitisation** — `path.normalize(reqPath).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/, "")`
 *    collapses dot-segments and strips leading parent-jumps; `path.join(ROOT, …)`
 *    maps `/` and `""` to `index.html`; the extension is lowercased once.
 * 2. **Traversal guard** — `isInsideRoot(ROOT, fp)` plus an explicit
 *    re-admission of `index.html`; violations get `403 Forbidden` via
 *    `sendText`.
 * 3. **Contentful fallbacks** (inside `fs.readFile`'s error callback):
 *    missing *images* (png/jpg/jpeg/webp/ico/svg) get an inline 64×64 SVG
 *    placeholder (a 200 response with `image/svg+xml`!); missing *pages*
 *    (`.html` or no extension) get `serveSPA(res)`; anything else gets
 *    `404 — ${reqPath}`.
 * 4. **Success** — `securityHeaders(res)`, then `writeHead(200)` with the
 *    MIME from `MIMES` (fallback `application/octet-stream`) and a cache
 *    policy: `no-cache` for `.html`, `public, max-age=3600` (one hour)
 *    otherwise.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `reqPath` — the decoded pathname (e.g. `/assets/css/base.css`).
 * - Files on disk under the project root: `assets/**`, fonts, `favicon`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Static bytes + headers, or one of the three fallbacks above.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Called from the main router's final `serveStatic(pathname, res)` branch and
 * from the SPA branch when the path has a known non-HTML extension. The
 * consumers are the `<link>`/`<img>`/`<script>` tags of `index.html`
 * (`tokens.css`, `base.css`, `components.css`, `home.css`, `browse.css`,
 * `performance-modes.css`, `/assets/icon/logo.webp`,
 * `/assets/icon/files-logo.webp`, all eight deferred scripts) and the
 * runtime-injected markup of `home.js`/`browse.js`/`autocomplete.js`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on `path`, `fs`, `isInsideRoot`, `MIMES`, `securityHeaders`,
 * `sendText`, `serveSPA`, and the inline SVG string constant embedded below.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The inline-SVG-on-404 trick is why the navbar logo carries an inline
 * `onerror="this.style.display='none'"` in `index.html` — the frontend has
 * its *own* missing-image fallback, and the two coexist (server fallback for
 * `<img>` loads that never reach JS handlers, `onerror` for robustness).
 * nginx implements the same "serve assets with long cache" concept via its
 * regex location (30d, `immutable`) — production caches longer than dev.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function serveStatic(reqPath, res) {
 *     const safe = path.normalize(reqPath).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/, "");
 *     const fp = path.join(ROOT, safe === "/" || safe === "" ? "index.html" : safe);
 *     const ext = path.extname(fp).toLowerCase();
 *
 *     if (!isInsideRoot(ROOT, fp) && path.resolve(fp) !== path.resolve(ROOT, "index.html")) {
 *         sendText(res, 403, "Forbidden");
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     fs.readFile(fp, (err, data) => {
 *         if (err) {
 *             if ([".png", ".jpg", ".jpeg", ".webp", ".ico", ".svg"].includes(ext)) {
 *                 const svg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="64" height="64" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1.5"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="3" opacity="0.3"/><circle cx="9" cy="9" r="2"/><path d="m21 15-3.086-3.086a2 2 0 0 0-2.828 0L6 21"/></svg>`;
 *                 res.writeHead(200, { "Content-Type": "image/svg+xml" });
 *                 res.end(svg);
 *                 return;
 *             }
 *
 *             if (!ext || ext === ".html") {
 *                 serveSPA(res);
 *                 return;
 *             }
 *
 *             sendText(res, 404, `404 — ${reqPath}`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         securityHeaders(res);
 *         res.writeHead(200, {
 *             "Content-Type": MIMES[ext] || "application/octet-stream",
 *             "Cache-Control": ext === ".html" ? "no-cache" : "public, max-age=3600",
 *         });
 *         res.end(data);
 *     });
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This is the file-serving heart of the app. Softening the traversal guard
 * exposes the *source tree* (including `lib/` and `server.js` if the router
 * blocklist is ever bypassed). Removing the SVG placeholder changes how the
 * UI looks under broken image URLs (home/browse cards would show browser
 * broken-image icons). Changing the one-hour `max-age` invalidates the
 * frontend's assumptions about asset freshness during deploys. Note the SVG
 * fallback deliberately answers **200** — that is what keeps `<img>` elements
 * rendering; changing it to 404 must be coordinated with the `onerror`
 * handler on the logo in `index.html`.
 *
 * @see index.html
 * @see assets/js/home.js
 * @see assets/js/browse.js
 * @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 * @see nginx.conf
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_streamdownload 🧩 Function: `streamDownload(req, res, fileInfo)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `serveStatic`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The big-box counter. Small answers go in envelopes; a 5-gigabyte disk image
 * goes into a stream — the receptionist starts rolling the box out the door
 * *immediately*, piece by piece, instead of trying to lift the whole thing at
 * once. And if the customer hangs up halfway, they stop rolling immediately
 * too.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * Builds headers from `fileInfo` (produced by `filesService.getDownloadTarget`,
 * which has already validated existence and file-ness): MIME from `MIMES`
 * (fallback octet-stream), exact `Content-Length` from `stat`, the RFC 5987
 * disposition from `contentDisposition`, `Cache-Control: private, max-age=0,
 * must-revalidate`, permissive CORS, and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`.
 * Three lifecycle details make it production-grade:
 * 1. **HEAD support** — headers only, `res.end()` with no body.
 * 2. **Security headers before streaming** — `securityHeaders(res)` then
 *    `writeHead`.
 * 3. **Backpressure & cancellation** — `fs.createReadStream(...).pipe(res)`
 *    honours TCP backpressure; `stream.on("error")` answers 500 only if
 *    headers have not been sent yet (`!res.headersSent`), otherwise it
 *    `res.destroy()`s mid-flight; `req.on("close")` destroys the stream so an
 *    aborted download stops disk reads.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `req` — used for method (`HEAD`) and the `close` (abort) event.
 * - `fileInfo` — `{ absPath, fileName, size, mtime }` from
 *   `lib/files-service.js`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * A streamed file with `Content-Disposition: attachment; ...`, or a 500 text
 * response on early read failure.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Triggered exclusively by the `/api/download` router branch. Frontend
 * producers of those URLs: `ResourceService.downloadHref()` used by
 * `home.js` (quick-links, search results, release cards), `browse.js` (file
 * rows), and `autocomplete.js` (`itemHref` for non-directory suggestions).
 * Because the SPA never fetches this URL with `fetch()`, the browser's
 * native download manager is the real consumer — which is why
 * `router.js`'s click interceptor explicitly skips file-extension links.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on `fs.createReadStream`, `path.extname`, `MIMES`,
 * `contentDisposition`, `securityHeaders`, `sendText`.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The `no-store` on JSON endpoints vs `private, must-revalidate` on downloads
 * mirrors the frontend cache split: `resource-service.js` caches *metadata*
 * in `sessionStorage` for 5 minutes but never caches *bytes*. `nginx.conf`
 * proxies this endpoint with `proxy_buffering off` and 3600s read/send
 * timeouts — the numbers match exactly so a slow multi-GB download is never
 * truncated by the proxy.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * function streamDownload(req, res, fileInfo) {
 *     const ext = path.extname(fileInfo.fileName).toLowerCase();
 *     const headers = {
 *         "Content-Type": MIMES[ext] || "application/octet-stream",
 *         "Content-Length": fileInfo.size,
 *         "Content-Disposition": contentDisposition(fileInfo.fileName),
 *         "Cache-Control": "private, max-age=0, must-revalidate",
 *         "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*",
 *         "X-Content-Type-Options": "nosniff",
 *     };
 *
 *     if (req.method === "HEAD") {
 *         res.writeHead(200, headers);
 *         res.end();
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     securityHeaders(res);
 *     res.writeHead(200, headers);
 *     const stream = fs.createReadStream(fileInfo.absPath);
 *     stream.on("error", () => {
 *         if (!res.headersSent) sendText(res, 500, "Failed to read file");
 *         else res.destroy();
 *     });
 *     req.on("close", () => stream.destroy());
 *     stream.pipe(res);
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * The `fileInfo` shape (`absPath`, `fileName`, `size`) is a contract with
 * `lib/files-service.js`'s `getDownloadTarget`. Switching `pipe(res)` to a
 * manual `data`/`end` loop without backpressure can balloon memory on large
 * `.iso`/`.qcow2`/`.vmdk` files — the exact artefacts this portal exists to
 * serve. Removing the `req.on("close")` handler makes aborted downloads keep
 * reading disk. Removing `res.headersSent` checking throws
 * `ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT` on mid-stream failures. And per `nginx.conf`, the
 * 3600s proxy timeouts assume downloads can legitimately run for an hour;
 * adding buffering (`res.end(buffer)`) or changing cache headers interacts
 * with that configuration.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/router.js
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_handler 🧩 Block: The Main Request Handler — `http.createServer(async (req, res) => …)`
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * After `streamDownload`; the largest single block in the file.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * This is the receptionist's actual brain — the big checklist they run
 * through for *every single visitor*, in the same order every time:
 * understand the request → handle special "may I pass?" pings → reject wrong
 * methods → refuse dangerous doors → answer API questions → hand out pages
 * → hand out closet items → and if anything explodes anywhere along the way,
 * apologise politely in JSON.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth) — Sub-blocks in exact order
 *
 * @par 🧩 1. Latency timer and URL parsing
 * Records `const start = Date.now()` for every log line, then parses
 * `req.url` into a `URL` (against the synthetic `http://localhost:${PORT}`
 * base) and percent-decodes `url.pathname` into `pathname`. Any parse or
 * decode failure (`URIError` from malformed `%` sequences) lands in the
 * `catch` with `sendText(res, 400, "Bad Request")`.
 *
 * @par 🧩 2. CORS preflight (OPTIONS)
 * Any `OPTIONS` request gets `204` with
 * `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, `Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD,
 * OPTIONS`, `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type`,
 * `Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400` (browsers cache the preflight for a day).
 * This is what lets the portal be embedded or driven from other origins even
 * though nginx only proxies `/api/`.
 *
 * @par 🧩 3. Method gate
 * Anything that is not `GET` or `HEAD` receives `405 Method Not Allowed`
 * *before* any routing happens — a quiet rejection of POST/PUT/DELETE
 * scanners.
 *
 * @par 🧩 4. Security blocklist
 * Three prefixes are refused with `403 Forbidden` + a log line:
 * `/.env` (secrets!), `/lib/` (server-side source: `files-service.js`,
 * `load-env.js`), `/node_modules/` (dependency source). This is the Node-side
 * twin of nginx's `location ~ /\.` deny rule — both layers must stay because
 * dev traffic skips nginx and production traffic skips neither.
 *
 * @par 🧩 5. `/api/health` + `/__health`
 * Calls the **synchronous** `filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT)` (safe
 * in a hot path: one `existsSync`/`statSync`, no awaits) and answers with
 * `{ status: "ok"|"degraded", service: "CloudAvid File Portal", uptime:
 * process.uptime(), timestamp, filesRootConfigured, filesRootReady }`.
 * `"ok"` requires configured ∧ exists ∧ isDirectory; anything else is
 * `"degraded"` (HTTP 200 either way, so uptime monitors alert on *payload
 * semantics*, not status codes).
 *
 * @par 🧩 6. `/api/categories`
 * `await filesService.listCategories(PROJECT_ROOT)` → array of category
 * objects (`name`, `path`, `type: "directory"`, `fileCount`, `folderCount`,
 * `mtime`, `latestModified`), sorted by name. Logged with the category count
 * and latency.
 *
 * @par 🧩 7. `/api/releases`
 * Reads `?limit=` (string or null; the service coerces and caps it), calls
 * `listLatestReleases(PROJECT_ROOT, limit)` → the newest files across all
 * categories with `{ name, path, category, type, size, mtime }`.
 *
 * @par 🧩 8. `/api/search`
 * Reads `?q=` (default `""`) and `?limit=`, calls
 * `searchEntries(PROJECT_ROOT, q, { limit })` → `{ results, scanned,
 * truncated }`. Consumers: `autocomplete.js` (limit 6, debounced) and
 * `home.js` (full search view).
 *
 * @par 🧩 9. `/api/files` and `/api/files/...`
 * Dual grammar: bare `/api/files` takes `?path=` from the query string;
 * `/api/files/<rel>` takes the tail via `relFromPrefix`. Empty `rel` lists
 * the category roots. Consumer: `ResourceService.listFiles()` → `browse.js`.
 *
 * @par 🧩 10. `/api/download` and `/api/download/...`
 * Same dual grammar; resolves via `getDownloadTarget(PROJECT_ROOT, rel)`
 * (which *throws* typed service errors for traversal/404/not-a-file — caught
 * by the outer catch) and hands the descriptor to `streamDownload`. This is
 * the only branch that streams bytes instead of JSON.
 *
 * @par 🧩 11. SPA routes
 * `/`, `/index.html`, `/browse`, and anything under `/browse/` route to the
 * app. A small wrinkle: if the path carries a *known non-HTML* extension
 * (`ext && MIMES[ext] && ext !== ".html"`), it is served by `serveStatic`
 * instead (logged with `static`), so `/browse/logo.svg` is an asset while
 * `/browse/ISOs/` is a client route. Everything else becomes `serveSPA`
 * (logged with `SPA`).
 *
 * @par 🧩 12. Static fallback
 * Any other path goes to `serveStatic(pathname, res)` (logged with `asset`).
 * Combined with the SPA branch this makes the server's page behaviour
 * identical to nginx's `try_files $uri $uri/ @spa`.
 *
 * @par 🧩 13. The error net
 * The whole routing chain sits in one `try`: any thrown service error maps
 * through `sendServiceError` (status from `err.httpStatus`, payload
 * `{error, code}`) and is logged with `err.code || err.message` and latency.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `req.url` (path + query), `req.method`.
 * - `process.env.FILES_ROOT` (read inside the service).
 * - The on-disk downloads tree pointed at by `FILES_ROOT`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * JSON for the five read endpoints; streamed bytes for downloads; HTML shell
 * for SPA routes; static bytes for assets; typed errors for everything that
 * fails.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward — who calls these endpoints, file by file
 * - `assets/js/services/resource-service.js` — the single frontend API
 *   client: `fetch("/api/categories")` in `getResources()`,
 *   `fetch("/api/files/" + …)` in `listFiles()`,
 *   `fetch("/api/search?" + params)` in `search()`,
 *   `fetch("/api/releases?limit=" + …)` in `getReleases()`, and
 *   `downloadHref()` building `/api/download/…` links. It adds its own
 *   5-minute `sessionStorage` cache (`cav_resources_cache`) on top of the
 *   `no-store` responses.
 * - `assets/js/home.js` — `getResources` (category grid), `getReleases(8)`
 *   (latest releases), `ResourceService.search(q, …)` (search view),
 *   `downloadHref`/`browseHref` for links.
 * - `assets/js/browse.js` — `listFiles(relPath)` per folder, `downloadHref`
 *   for file rows, `browseHref` for folder navigation.
 * - `assets/js/autocomplete.js` — debounced `ResourceService.search(q,
 *   {limit: 6})`, `Router.navigate` for folders, `downloadHref` for files.
 * - `nginx.conf` — proxies `location /api/` → `http://127.0.0.1:3000` with
 *   `proxy_buffering off` and 3600s timeouts; injects
 *   `Cache-Control: no-store... always` on API responses.
 * - External uptime monitors — `/api/health` (or `/__health`).
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward — what this handler depends on
 * - `lib/files-service.js`: `peekRootSync`, `listCategories`,
 *   `listLatestReleases`, `searchEntries`, `listPath`, `getDownloadTarget`
 *   — every one of the six functions of its public API.
 * - This file's own helpers: `sendText`, `sendJSON`, `sendServiceError`,
 *   `streamDownload`, `serveSPA`, `serveStatic`, `relFromPrefix`, `log`.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The route vocabulary here (`/`, `/browse/...`) is mirrored by
 * `assets/js/router.js`'s `resolveRoute()` — the two files form the
 * server/client routing contract that `nginx.conf`'s `@spa` fallback
 * completes. The dual grammar of `/api/files` and `/api/download` (`?path=`
 * query *or* path segments) is a server-side affordance: today's frontend
 * producers (verified across `assets/js/`) use only the *segment* form via
 * `encodeRelPath()`/`downloadHref()`/`listFiles()`; the `?path=` form is
 * reserved surface for tools, scripts and `curl` workflows.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
 *     const start = Date.now();
 *     let pathname;
 *     let url;
 *
 *     try {
 *         url = new URL(req.url, `http://localhost:${PORT}`);
 *         pathname = decodeURIComponent(url.pathname);
 *     } catch {
 *         sendText(res, 400, "Bad Request");
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     if (req.method === "OPTIONS") {
 *         res.writeHead(204, {
 *             "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": "*",
 *             "Access-Control-Allow-Methods": "GET, HEAD, OPTIONS",
 *             "Access-Control-Allow-Headers": "Content-Type",
 *             "Access-Control-Max-Age": "86400",
 *         });
 *         res.end();
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     if (req.method !== "GET" && req.method !== "HEAD") {
 *         sendText(res, 405, "Method Not Allowed");
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     const blocked = pathname === "/.env" || pathname.startsWith("/lib/") || pathname.startsWith("/node_modules/");
 *     if (blocked) {
 *         sendText(res, 403, "Forbidden");
 *         log(req.method, 403, pathname);
 *         return;
 *     }
 *
 *     try {
 *         if (pathname === "/__health" || pathname === "/api/health") {
 *             const peek = filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT);
 *             sendJSON(res, 200, {
 *                 status: peek.configured && peek.exists && peek.isDirectory ? "ok" : "degraded",
 *                 service: "CloudAvid File Portal",
 *                 uptime: process.uptime(),
 *                 timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
 *                 filesRootConfigured: peek.configured,
 *                 filesRootReady: Boolean(peek.exists && peek.isDirectory),
 *             });
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (pathname === "/api/categories") {
 *             const categories = await filesService.listCategories(PROJECT_ROOT);
 *             sendJSON(res, 200, categories);
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `${categories.length} categories · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (pathname === "/api/releases") {
 *             const limit = url.searchParams.get("limit");
 *             const releases = await filesService.listLatestReleases(PROJECT_ROOT, limit);
 *             sendJSON(res, 200, releases);
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `${releases.length} · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (pathname === "/api/search") {
 *             const q = url.searchParams.get("q") || "";
 *             const limit = url.searchParams.get("limit");
 *             const data = await filesService.searchEntries(PROJECT_ROOT, q, { limit });
 *             sendJSON(res, 200, data);
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `${data.results.length} · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (pathname === "/api/files" || pathname.startsWith("/api/files/")) {
 *             const rel = pathname === "/api/files"
 *                 ? (url.searchParams.get("path") || "")
 *                 : relFromPrefix(pathname, "/api/files");
 *             const entries = await filesService.listPath(PROJECT_ROOT, rel);
 *             sendJSON(res, 200, entries);
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `${entries.length} items · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (pathname === "/api/download" || pathname.startsWith("/api/download/")) {
 *             const rel = pathname === "/api/download"
 *                 ? (url.searchParams.get("path") || "")
 *                 : relFromPrefix(pathname, "/api/download");
 *             const fileInfo = await filesService.getDownloadTarget(PROJECT_ROOT, rel);
 *             streamDownload(req, res, fileInfo);
 *             log(req.method, 200, pathname, `${fileInfo.fileName} · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         if (
 *             pathname === "/" ||
 *             pathname === "/index.html" ||
 *             pathname === "/browse" ||
 *             pathname.startsWith("/browse/")
 *         ) {
 *             const ext = path.extname(pathname).toLowerCase();
 *             if (ext && MIMES[ext] && ext !== ".html") {
 *                 serveStatic(pathname, res);
 *                 log("GET", 200, pathname, `static · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *                 return;
 *             }
 *             serveSPA(res);
 *             log("GET", 200, pathname, `SPA · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *             return;
 *         }
 *
 *         serveStatic(pathname, res);
 *         log("GET", 200, pathname, `asset · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *     } catch (err) {
 *         const status = err.httpStatus || 500;
 *         sendServiceError(res, err);
 *         log(req.method, status, pathname, `${err.code || err.message} · ${Date.now() - start}ms`);
 *     }
 * });
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * This handler is the **single point of coupling** for the entire stack:
 * every `if` block's pathname is a promise made to the frontend (see the
 * fetch URLs in `resource-service.js`), to `nginx.conf` (proxy rules), and
 * to the health checks. Reordering the API branches is safe (they are
 * mutually exclusive exact matches), but *reordering the method/blocklist
 * gates below the API routing* would let POSTs and `/.env` reach the API —
 * a genuine security regression. Deleting the `/__health` alias breaks any
 * monitor configured against it. And note the dual grammar of
 * `/api/files` + `/api/download`: removing the *segment* form breaks the
 * frontend outright (every URL is built by `encodeRelPath`/`downloadHref`),
 * while removing the `?path=` form breaks no current frontend code — but it
 * is documented API surface (see the startup banner's
 * `/api/files/<category>` style and any scripted/`curl` consumers), so
 * deprecate it deliberately, never silently.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see lib/load-env.js
 * @see assets/js/services/resource-service.js
 * @see assets/js/home.js
 * @see assets/js/browse.js
 * @see assets/js/autocomplete.js
 * @see assets/js/router.js
 * @see nginx.conf
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_listen 🧩 Block: `server.listen(PORT, HOST, …)` — Startup Banner
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * Immediately after `http.createServer`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The moment the reception desk opens, it pins a big friendly sign on the
 * door: "we are open, here is where the rooms are, and here is whether the
 * archive room actually exists today." That last bit matters — if the
 * manager forgot to write `FILES_ROOT` on the rulebook, the sign turns
 * yellow and tells you *exactly* what is missing instead of letting the
 * server limp along mysteriously.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * The callback runs once the socket is bound. It calls
 * `filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT)` and converts the three failure
 * dimensions into a colour-coded status line: not configured → yellow
 * "FILES_ROOT is not set"; missing path → yellow "FILES_ROOT path was not
 * found"; not a directory → yellow "FILES_ROOT is not a directory";
 * otherwise green "FILES_ROOT is configured". It then prints the boxed
 * banner with all six canonical endpoint URLs. Note the deliberate choice to
 * **not** exit on a bad FILES_ROOT — the server still starts, health
 * reports `"degraded"`, and API calls will answer typed 503s from
 * `lib/files-service.js`; this makes misconfiguration observable rather than
 * fatal, which is friendlier to container orchestration that restarts
 * crash-looping processes.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - `PORT`, `HOST` constants; `FILES_ROOT` (via `peekRootSync` reading
 *   `process.env`).
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Stdout: the banner, the FILES_ROOT status, the hint
 * "Set FILES_ROOT in .env — no filesystem paths are hardcoded." and
 * "Press Ctrl+C to stop".
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Triggered by `npm start` (`package.json`) in dev, or by the process
 * manager in production behind `nginx.conf`.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on `filesService.peekRootSync` — the only **synchronous** export
 * of the service, chosen so the banner never needs async plumbing.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The banner's endpoint list is a human-readable mirror of the router:
 * `/api/health`, `/api/categories`, `/api/files/<category>`,
 * `/api/download/<category>/<file>`, `/api/search?q=`, `/api/releases`.
 * The `.env` hint mirrors `.env.example`'s comments verbatim in spirit.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * server.listen(PORT, HOST, () => {
 *     const peek = filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT);
 *     const rootState = !peek.configured
 *         ? `${c.yellow}FILES_ROOT is not set${c.reset}`
 *         : !peek.exists
 *             ? `${c.yellow}FILES_ROOT path was not found${c.reset}`
 *             : !peek.isDirectory
 *                 ? `${c.yellow}FILES_ROOT is not a directory${c.reset}`
 *                 : `${c.green}FILES_ROOT is configured${c.reset}`;
 *
 *     console.log(`
 * ${c.cyan}${c.bold}
 *   ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
 *   ║     CloudAvid File Portal — File API Server      ║
 *   ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════╝${c.reset}
 *
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Local:       ${c.bold}http://localhost:${PORT}/${c.reset}
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Health:      http://localhost:${PORT}/api/health
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Categories:  http://localhost:${PORT}/api/categories
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Files:       http://localhost:${PORT}/api/files/<category>
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Download:    http://localhost:${PORT}/api/download/<category>/<file>
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Search:      http://localhost:${PORT}/api/search?q=
 *   ${c.green}➜${c.reset}  Releases:    http://localhost:${PORT}/api/releases
 *
 *   ${rootState}
 *   ${c.dim}Set FILES_ROOT in .env — no filesystem paths are hardcoded.${c.reset}
 *   ${c.dim}Press Ctrl+C to stop${c.reset}
 * `);
 * });
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Anything parsing this banner (container startup probes grepping for
 * "configured", documentation screenshots, onboarding scripts) breaks on
 * wording changes. More subtly: `peekRootSync` runs *once*, at startup — if
 * `FILES_ROOT` becomes valid *after* boot (a volume mount arriving late in
 * containers), the banner stays yellow while requests succeed. Do not "fix"
 * that by making the server exit on a bad root; the current
 * start-anyway + degraded-health behaviour is the intended contract.
 *
 * @see lib/files-service.js
 * @see package.json
 * @see .env.example
 * @see nginx.conf
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_sigint 🧩 Block: The SIGINT Shutdown Handler
 *
 * @par 📍 Location
 * The final statement of `server.js`.
 *
 * @par 🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
 * The closing-time ritual. When the operator presses Ctrl+C, the receptionist
 * doesn't just run out the door mid-sentence — they finish the sentence,
 * politely say "Shutting down file portal…", lock the door, and *then* turn
 * off the lights.
 *
 * @par ⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
 * `process.on("SIGINT", …)` intercepts Ctrl+C (and the SIGINT signal sent by
 * container stop sequences), prints a yellow notice, and calls
 * `server.close(() => process.exit(0))`. `server.close` stops accepting
 * **new** connections but lets in-flight requests (notably active
 * `streamDownload` pipes!) finish before the callback fires and exits with
 * code 0. Edge case worth knowing: if a download is streaming and the client
 * never disconnects, `close`'s callback can be delayed indefinitely — that
 * is precisely why `streamDownload` registers `req.on("close")` to destroy
 * streams on client disconnects, and why an operator may still need a
 * second Ctrl+C (which, with this handler installed, is captured again and
 * merely re-runs the same path) or a SIGKILL from the orchestrator.
 *
 * @par 📥 Inputs
 * - The OS signal `SIGINT`.
 *
 * @par 📤 Outputs
 * Stdout notice; process exit code 0 after graceful drain.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Upward
 * Depended upon by deployment hygiene: `nginx.conf`'s 3600s proxy timeouts
 * imply long-lived download connections, and graceful drain is what makes
 * `systemctl restart`-style operations not truncate user downloads.
 *
 * @par 🔗 Trace Downward
 * Depends on `server` (the `http.Server` instance) and `console`.
 *
 * @par 🤝 Trace Sideways
 * The frontend has no equivalent — browsers own their lifecycle. The closest
 * analogue is `resource-service.js`'s `clearCache()`, another explicit
 * "clean up before leaving" utility.
 *
 * @par 💻 Actual Code
 * ```javascript
 * process.on("SIGINT", () => {
 *     console.log(`\n${c.yellow}Shutting down file portal…${c.reset}`);
 *     server.close(() => process.exit(0));
 * });
 * ```
 *
 * ⚠️ Breaking Change Warning
 * Removing this handler reverts Ctrl+C to Node's default: immediate
 * termination, truncated streams, and the `nginx.conf` upstream briefly
 * serving connection-refused 502s during every restart. Replacing
 * `process.exit(0)` with a nonzero code can make orchestrators treat planned
 * restarts as crashes. If you later add `SIGTERM` support (recommended for
 * containers), reuse this same close-and-exit body — two divergent shutdown
 * paths is how zombie downloads happen.
 *
 * @see nginx.conf
 * @see server_streamdownload
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_lifecycle 🔄 End-to-End Lifecycle: One Download, Touched by Six Files
 *
 * The sequence below follows a real user action — clicking a file card in
 * the Browse view — through every layer of the project, so each `@see`
 * target in this document appears in context.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph download_sequence {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   click    [label="user clicks file card\\n(assets/js/browse.js)", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *   href     [label="ResourceService.downloadHref(relPath)\\n→ /api/download/<encoded path>\\n(assets/js/services/resource-service.js)", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *   skip     [label="router.js click interceptor sees file extension\\n→ stands aside (no SPA navigation)", fillcolor="#E0E7FF"];
 *   nginx    [label="nginx.conf: location /api/\\nproxy_pass → 127.0.0.1:3000\\nbuffering off · 3600s timeouts", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   handler  [label="server.js main handler\\n/api/download/ branch\\nrelFromPrefix → getDownloadTarget", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   service  [label="lib/files-service.js\\ngetRoot → resolveInsideRoot\\n(FILES_ROOT, traversal guard)\\nstat → fileInfo", fillcolor="#CFFAFE"];
 *   stream   [label="server.js streamDownload\\nMIMES + contentDisposition\\nfs.createReadStream → pipe(res)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   browser  [label="Browser download manager saves the file\\n(Unicode name preserved via RFC 5987)", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   error    [label="typed service error?\\nsendServiceError → {error, code}\\nparseError in resource-service.js surfaces it in the UI", fillcolor="#FEE2E2"];
 *
 *   click -> href -> skip -> nginx -> handler -> service;
 *   service -> stream [label="fileInfo ok"];
 *   service -> error [label="403 PATH_TRAVERSAL / 404 NOT_FOUND / …", style=dashed];
 *   stream -> browser [label="binary stream"];
 *   handler -> error [style=dashed];
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @see dox/main/index.dox
 * @see dox/main/browse.dox
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section server_maintainer 🛠️ Maintainer's Cheat Sheet
 *
 * | If you change… | …then check these, or this breaks |
 * |---|---|
 * | Any `/api/*` pathname | `assets/js/services/resource-service.js` (the only fetch client), `nginx.conf` proxy rules |
 * | Response JSON shapes | `parseError()`/`mapCategory()`/`listFiles()` consumers in the frontend; error cards in `home.js`/`browse.js` |
 * | `MIMES` table | `serveStatic` fallbacks, `streamDownload` types, SPA-route extension guard, `router.js` `fileExtensions` regex |
 * | `error`/`code` contract | `sendServiceError` ↔ `serviceError()` in `lib/files-service.js` ↔ `parseError()` |
 * | `PORT` default | `nginx.conf` `proxy_pass`, startup banner, deployment env |
 * | `index.html` path/name | `serveSPA`, all SPA deep links, `nginx.conf` `@spa` |
 * | `FILES_ROOT` handling | `.env`/`.env.example`, `peekRootSync` banner state, all 503 errors from the service |
 * | Static cache policy | Deploy freshness of `assets/**` vs nginx's 30d `immutable` |
 * | Shutdown behaviour | Active download streams, nginx 502 window during restarts |
 *
 * @note Everything above is verified against the repository as of this
 * documentation pass: `server.js`, `lib/files-service.js`, `lib/load-env.js`,
 * `assets/js/services/resource-service.js`, `assets/js/router.js`,
 * `assets/js/home.js`, `assets/js/browse.js`, `assets/js/autocomplete.js`,
 * `index.html`, `nginx.conf`, `package.json`, `.env.example`. No APIs or
 * behaviours are documented that do not exist in those files.
 *
 * @author ShaiyaAzizi
 * @version 1.0.0
 */